Title:
pyTGTdeleg — Remote Kerberos TGT extraction via MSSQL (SQL CLR tgtdeleg)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
pyTGTdeleg is a Python tool that remotely extracts a usable forwarded Kerberos TGT from a domain-joined Microsoft SQL Server by abusing the Kerberos delegation “tgtdeleg” technique. It requires MSSQL sysadmin (often `sa`) access and executes a custom SQL CLR assembly inside `sqlservr.exe` to trigger SSPI delegation (`ISC_REQ_DELEGATE`) against an SPN on a host with unconstrained delegation (commonly a DC). The CLR code retrieves the service ticket session key via `LsaCallAuthenticationPackage`, returns the raw SPNEGO token + key to the client, and the Python side parses/decrypts the AP-REQ authenticator to recover the embedded KRB-CRED and output a `.ccache`. The approach is designed for low-interaction execution (no cmd/powershell, no disk writes) and includes automatic cleanup of SQL artifacts and CLR configuration changes. It’s primarily useful for red teams/pentesters to pivot from SQL sysadmin to domain Kerberos abuse using standard Kerberos tooling (Impacket). The key value is turning database admin access into portable Kerberos credentials without deploying an agent or writing binaries to disk.

Technical Focus:
- Kerberos delegation abuse (tgtdeleg / forwarded TGT extraction)
- MSSQL SQL CLR in-process execution (`sqlservr.exe`) via `CREATE ASSEMBLY`
- SSPI APIs: `AcquireCredentialsHandle`, `InitializeSecurityContext` with `ISC_REQ_DELEGATE`
- LSA interaction: `LsaCallAuthenticationPackage` and Kerberos ticket cache/session key retrieval
- SPNEGO / GSS-API parsing, AP-REQ authenticator decryption, KRB-CRED extraction
- `.ccache` generation and Impacket Kerberos (-k) workflows

Use Cases:
- Extract a service account TGT from a compromised domain-joined MSSQL server using only sysadmin creds
- Kerberos-based lateral movement using the recovered `.ccache` (SMB, WMI/PSExec equivalents via Impacket)
- Post-exploitation credential pivoting without spawning child processes or writing payloads to disk
- Follow-on AD attacks when the SQL service account has privileges (e.g., DCSync if replication rights exist)
- Kerberos ticket operations (GetST, Kerberoasting requests) using the extracted identity

Keywords:
Kerberos, TGT, tgtdeleg, delegation, unconstrained delegation, MSSQL, SQL Server, SQL CLR, CREATE ASSEMBLY, sqlservr.exe, SSPI, AcquireCredentialsHandle, InitializeSecurityContext, ISC_REQ_DELEGATE, SPNEGO, GSS-API, AP-REQ, Authenticator, KRB-CRED, LsaCallAuthenticationPackage, ticket cache, session key, ccache, Impacket, Rubeus, DCSync, secretsdump, GetST, GetUserSPNs, psexec, Protected Users, NOT_DELEGATED